  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Combining Simple Transform Pairs",
    "lesson_core_move": "Combine known one-sided Laplace transform pairs by matching each term in a time-domain sum or s-domain expression to its pair and applying linearity to form the corresponding composite transform or inverse transform.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Combining Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Combining Simple Transform Pairs",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided Laplace transform linearity rule with controlled term matching: identify the exact time-domain terms first, then map each one to a known transform before writing any composite result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For this lesson, the core action is matching pairs and carrying coefficients and signs unchanged across domains. Start from the direct goal, $\\mathcal{L}\\{a_1x_1(t)+a_2x_2(t)\\}=a_1X_1(s)+a_2X_2(s)$, then execute each term in order.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{a_1x_1(t)+a_2x_2(t)\\}=a_1X_1(s)+a_2X_2(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case from this step: $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)+2u(t)$. Match $e^{-t}u(t)$ to $\\frac{1}{s+1}$ and $2u(t)$ to $\\frac{2}{s}$, then combine by keeping the coefficient $2$ attached to the constant term and $+$ signs attached to their terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the same idea in the inverse direction when the expression is given in the $s$-domain. Match each known transform term to its time signal and add the reconstructed terms with the original multipliers and signs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{A_1X_1(s)+A_2X_2(s)\\}=A_1x_1(t)+A_2x_2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Do not simplify or recombine the sum before matching: the highest-frequency error here is merging terms too early and then misplacing a sign or coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This topic uses the same move in two directions: matching in the time domain to build $X(s)$, and matching in the $s$-domain to build $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Combining Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Matching Time-Domain Sum Terms to Known Laplace Pairs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the one-sided Laplace transform of $x(t)=3u(t)-2e^{-2t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by reading the expression as a termwise match, not a single combined transform. The cue is this form: $x(t)=3u(t)+(-2)e^{-2t}u(t)$, so each add/subtract term must be matched separately to a known one-sided pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the first term first: $3u(t)$ maps from $u(t)$, whose one-sided transform is $\\frac{1}{s}$, so $3u(t)$ maps to $\\frac{3}{s}$. Keep the scalar with that term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the second term next with the sign carried through: $e^{-2t}u(t)$ maps to $\\frac{1}{s+2}$, so $-2e^{-2t}u(t)$ maps to $-\\frac{2}{s+2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{3u(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s}, \\quad \\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{-2e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=-\\frac{2}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply one-sided linearity and write the composite result directly as the weighted sum: $X(s)=\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+2}$. Leave it in this separated term form for the next steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Combining Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Applying Coefficients and Signs to Build a Composite Transform",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $X(s)$ for $x(t)=2e^{-t}u(t)-\\frac{1}{2}u(t)+3e^{-4t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same cue as before: treat $x(t)$ as a sum of independent causal terms and apply one-sided linearity term-by-term before forming one final expression. Here $x(t)=2e^{-t}u(t)-\\frac{1}{2}u(t)+3e^{-4t}u(t)$ has three terms to match.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match each term independently. From $e^{-t}u(t)\\leftrightarrow\\frac{1}{s+1}$, $u(t)\\leftrightarrow\\frac{1}{s}$, and $e^{-4t}u(t)\\leftrightarrow\\frac{1}{s+4}$, so the transforms are ready to receive each coefficient and sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{e^{-t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+1},\\quad \\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s},\\quad \\mathcal{L}_{+}\\{e^{-4t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply coefficients and signs directly to each numerator: $2e^{-t}u(t)\\leftrightarrow \\frac{2}{s+1}$, $-\\frac{1}{2}u(t)\\leftrightarrow -\\frac{1}{2s}$, and $3e^{-4t}u(t)\\leftrightarrow \\frac{3}{s+4}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Assemble the weighted transform sum in separated form so coefficient errors stay visible: $X(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{2s}+\\frac{3}{s+4}$. This is the correct final structure for this section; simplification is not required yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Combining Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Matching s-Domain Sum Terms to Known Inverse Pairs",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ for $X(s)=\\frac{3}{s}+\\frac{2}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+4}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the inverse direction of the same matching move: identify each separated term in $X(s)$ first, then invert each term independently. The cue is to classify each piece as $\\frac{1}{s}$, $\\frac{1}{s+a}$, or a signed variant of that form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Split the expression as $\\frac{3}{s}+\\frac{2}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+4}$. This is one-to-one matching to the inverse templates with no extra algebra yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\right\\}=u(t),\\quad \\mathcal{L}_{+}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+a}\\right\\}=e^{-at}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the inverse rule term by term: $\\frac{3}{s}\\leftrightarrow 3u(t)$, $\\frac{2}{s+1}\\leftrightarrow 2e^{-t}u(t)$, and $-\\frac{1}{s+4}\\leftrightarrow -e^{-4t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3u(t)+2e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-4t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Recombine the inverse terms with the same additive structure and preserved signs: $x(t)=3u(t)+2e^{-t}u(t)-e^{-4t}u(t)$. This keeps the mapping visible for the next practice question set.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Combining Simple Transform Pairs",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recombining Inverse Pair Terms into a Time-Domain Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(t)$ for $X(s)=\\frac{5}{s+2}-\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+2}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use inverse matching as a terminal move: each term in $X(s)$ maps separately, then matching time-domain terms are recombined with linearity preserved.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Split the expression into identifiable parts: $\\frac{5}{s+2}$, $-\\frac{3}{s}$, and $-\\frac{2}{s+2}$. This is a controlled repeat of $s+2$ that checks coefficient grouping before final simplification.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}_{+}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+a}\\right\\}=e^{-at}u(t),\\quad \\mathcal{L}_{+}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\right\\}=u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Invert each term: $\\frac{5}{s+2}\\leftrightarrow 5e^{-2t}u(t)$, $-\\frac{3}{s}\\leftrightarrow -3u(t)$, and $-\\frac{2}{s+2}\\leftrightarrow -2e^{-2t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=5e^{-2t}u(t)-3u(t)-2e^{-2t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Recombine like terms and keep one-sided support explicit: $x(t)=\\left(5-2\\right)e^{-2t}u(t)-3u(t)$, so the final form is $x(t)=3e^{-2t}u(t)-3u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
